analysis:
The PR changes only docs/architecture/inference.md: it bumps the "Last updated" date and rewrites the GLM5Next video paragraph near lines 810-829 on the head side. The new text says variable-frame-rate MP4/QuickTime tracks are indexed at i / nominalFrameRate instead of being refused, and that processor refusals are deterministic 400s. The supplied units are the unchanged base text at lines 1611-2365. They contain no changed lines, so I cannot see the changed excerpt in the numbered text.

The patch is documentation-only and alters no code, configuration or controls. Any change in code behavior would live outside the supplied evidence; the doc describes it only in prose, so I have not verified it.

The documented behavior change is that VFR video is now accepted rather than rejected. Threat-model relevance is limited to TB-007 and T-050 (media handling). The doc keeps the existing safeguards:
- the 512 MiB decoded-frame cap (`maximumDecodedBytes`);
- the 28,532-token prepared-pixel cap;
- exclusion of media from prefix lookup and capture, so there is no cross-tenant KV reuse and T-041 is unaffected;
- memory-reservation checks through `GLM5NextVisionBudget`.

Remaining question for integration: whether the GLM5NextProcessor code change that accepts VFR video still enforces the frame-count and memory caps, and whether it avoids temp-file plaintext exposure (T-050). The supplied evidence does not show the implementing code, so this is a coverage limit, not a finding. No security regression is identified from the documentation change itself.
